// Hot swap in the REPL break loop: what works today of the break-loop
// milestone (`break_loop.transcript` is the fuller target).
//
// `raymarch.loko`'s `shade` method produces a negative brightness for rays
// that miss the sphere, and `check-sample` signals `<bad-sample>`, which
// nothing handles. The run stops with every frame live. Fixing `shade`
// compiles the session again and installs it as a new code unit (`u1`)
// beside the running one (`u0`): only the `shade` frame runs code the edit
// replaced. `retry frame:1` unwinds to the `shade` call and makes it again
// with the new method, so `main` returns 1.0 + 0.5 + 0.0 while `pixel`,
// `render` and `main` finish on the code they started with. A new run takes
// the new code from the start. A later scenario shows the edits the break
// loop refuses because suspended frames rely on the old definitions, later
// ones break on failed runtime checks, and the last ones on a send that
// finds no applicable method.
//
// Format: see `tests/support/transcript.rs`.

=== repl: edit a method under a suspended signal and retry its frame

> :load tests/fixtures/break_loop/raymarch.loko
loaded tests/fixtures/break_loop/raymarch.loko (10 forms)
> :run main
break [1]: no handler for <bad-sample>; 6 frames suspended, nothing unwound
frames (innermost first):
  frame:0 check-sample  u0:f0
  frame:1 shade#method0  u0:f4
  frame:2 shade#dispatch  u0:f9
  frame:3 pixel  u0:f1
  frame:4 render  u0:f2
  frame:5 main  u0:f3
restarts:
  restart:0 skip-pixel()  the handle site's recovery
  restart:1 use-value(<double-float>)  the signal returns the value
  restart:2 retry(frame)  call a frame's function again, with current definitions
  restart:3 abort()  unwind to top level, running cleanups
choose with :restart <n|name> [args...], or :edit <form-id> to fix a definition

// A broken edit is rejected and changes nothing; the fixed one installs.
> :edit form:f6
editing form:f6; enter a replacement definition
> define method shade(s :: <sphere>, distance :: <double-float>) => <double-float>
>   // A ray that misses the sphere is black, not negatively bright.
>   check-sample(max(0, 1.0 - distance / s.radius), distance)
> end;
! diagnostics:
!   Error sem.function-argument-type-mismatch 1091..1092 function `max` argument 1 expects <float> but got <integer>
! edit rejected; the definitions and break [1] are unchanged
> define method shade(s :: <sphere>, distance :: <double-float>) => <double-float>
>   // A ray that misses the sphere is black, not negatively bright.
>   check-sample(max(0.0, 1.0 - distance / s.radius), distance)
> end;
installed the edit as a new code unit; still running replaced code:
  frame:1 shade#method0  u0:f4  superseded by u1:f4
> :frames
frames (innermost first):
  frame:0 check-sample  u0:f0
  frame:1 shade#method0  u0:f4  superseded by u1:f4
  frame:2 shade#dispatch  u0:f9
  frame:3 pixel  u0:f1
  frame:4 render  u0:f2
  frame:5 main  u0:f3

// Retry the shade frame with the new method: 1.0 + 0.5 + 0.0.
> :restart retry frame:1
1.5
> :run main
1.5

// Bring the bug back, then resume the signal with a typed value:
// 1.0 + 0.5 + 0.25. A string is refused and the break keeps waiting.
> :reload
reloaded tests/fixtures/break_loop/raymarch.loko (10 forms)
> :run main
break [1]: no handler for <bad-sample>; 6 frames suspended, nothing unwound
frames (innermost first):
  frame:0 check-sample  u0:f0
  frame:1 shade#method0  u0:f4
  frame:2 shade#dispatch  u0:f9
  frame:3 pixel  u0:f1
  frame:4 render  u0:f2
  frame:5 main  u0:f3
restarts:
  restart:0 skip-pixel()  the handle site's recovery
  restart:1 use-value(<double-float>)  the signal returns the value
  restart:2 retry(frame)  call a frame's function again, with current definitions
  restart:3 abort()  unwind to top level, running cleanups
choose with :restart <n|name> [args...], or :edit <form-id> to fix a definition
> :restart use-value "dark"
! error: use-value expects <double-float>, got "dark"; break [1] is still waiting
> :restart use-value 0.25
1.75

// Abort unwinds everything, running `render`'s cleanup on the way out.
> :run main
break [1]: no handler for <bad-sample>; 6 frames suspended, nothing unwound
frames (innermost first):
  frame:0 check-sample  u0:f0
  frame:1 shade#method0  u0:f4
  frame:2 shade#dispatch  u0:f9
  frame:3 pixel  u0:f1
  frame:4 render  u0:f2
  frame:5 main  u0:f3
restarts:
  restart:0 skip-pixel()  the handle site's recovery
  restart:1 use-value(<double-float>)  the signal returns the value
  restart:2 retry(frame)  call a frame's function again, with current definitions
  restart:3 abort()  unwind to top level, running cleanups
choose with :restart <n|name> [args...], or :edit <form-id> to fix a definition
> :restart abort
! runtime error: aborted by the condition chooser
> :frames
no suspended computation
> :quit

// Suspended frames were compiled against the definitions they started
// with, so an edit must keep what they rely on. `shapes.loko`'s `main` reads
// `make-shape`'s result as a `<circle>` and `measure` reads its parameter
// as one. Editing `main` keeps its contract (`() => <integer>`) and installs.
// Retyping `measure`'s parameter installs too, since functions are called
// directly within their unit, but retrying the old `measure` frame would
// hand its `<circle>` to the new body, so that retry is refused. Making
// `make-shape` return a `<square>` is refused outright: old frames send to
// the newest methods. Both refusals leave the break waiting, and the old
// frames finish on their own code: 10 + 3. A new run takes every edit.

=== repl: an edit that breaks what suspended frames rely on is refused

> :load tests/fixtures/break_loop/shapes.loko
loaded tests/fixtures/break_loop/shapes.loko (8 forms)
> :run main
break [1]: no handler for <oops>; 3 frames suspended, nothing unwound
frames (innermost first):
  frame:0 pause  u0:f0
  frame:1 measure  u0:f1
  frame:2 main  u0:f2
restarts:
  restart:0 use-value(<integer>)  the signal returns the value
  restart:1 retry(frame)  call a frame's function again, with current definitions
  restart:2 abort()  unwind to top level, running cleanups
choose with :restart <n|name> [args...], or :edit <form-id> to fix a definition
> :edit form:f7
editing form:f7; enter a replacement definition
> define function main() => <integer>
>   0
> end;
installed the edit as a new code unit; still running replaced code:
  frame:2 main  u0:f2  superseded by u1:f2
> :edit form:f6
editing form:f6; enter a replacement definition
> define function measure(s :: <square>) => <integer>
>   s.side
> end;
installed the edit as a new code unit; still running replaced code:
  frame:1 measure  u0:f1  superseded by u2:f1  (cannot retry: measure would take <square> as argument 1 where callers pass <circle>)
  frame:2 main  u0:f2  superseded by u2:f2
> :restart retry frame:1
! error: frame:1 cannot be retried with the edited code: measure would take <square> as argument 1 where callers pass <circle>; break [1] is still waiting
> :edit form:f4
editing form:f4; enter a replacement definition
> define method make-shape(n :: <integer>)
>   make(<square>, side: n)
> end;
! error: the edit is accepted for new runs but not installed here: cannot replace running code: make-shape would return <square> as result 1 where callers expect <circle>
> :restart use-value 10
13
> :run main
0
> :quit

// A failed runtime check signals a `<type-error>`, and an unhandled one
// breaks like any condition. The break shows what the condition reports:
// its stable code, the expected type, the rejected value, and the site,
// here the id of the check's typing decision. `use-value` supplies what
// the checked conversion produces instead; a value of another type is
// refused and the break keeps waiting. A cast continues with an object it
// checks again. A literal cannot
// name an object, so a failed cast is fixed by editing the definition and
// retrying its frame instead.

=== repl: a failed check breaks with its type error and continues with use-value

> :load tests/fixtures/break_loop/checks.loko
loaded tests/fixtures/break_loop/checks.loko (7 forms)
> :run main
break [1]: no handler for <type-error>; 2 frames suspended, nothing unwound
type error: code=type-error.range expected=<i32> value=5000000000 site=typing:def34e752e9bedb7b3c:s0
frames (innermost first):
